  "chain": "corrosion bonding to seat (simmering, low stellite) [cause B] -> pilot valve setpoint drift high (0004: +5.37%) -> TS 3.4.3 setpoint band violation [backup MSRV Actuation Logic]; setpoint spring relaxation over time [cause B] -> setpoint drift low (0019/0031/0179: -3.17% to -5.73%) -> TS 3.4.3 setpoint band violation [backup MSRV Actuation Logic]",
  "golden_questions": [
    "Q1",
    "Q3",
    "Q4",
    "Q14"
  ],
  "notes": "Two parallel, physically distinct failure mechanisms modeled separately per rule 5: corrosion bonding/simmering (valve -0004, drifted high) and setpoint spring relaxation (valves -0019, -0031, -0179, drifted low), both attributed to the same official Cause B (Design/Manufacturing/Installation) reflecting inherent Target Rock two-stage pilot valve design susceptibility to setpoint drift. MSRV Automatic Actuation Logic is modeled as an operable backup because the narrative states it 'provides defense in depth' and would receive electrical open signals independent of mechanical setpoint drift during a pressurization transient -- distinguished from pure design-context language. No SSC was inoperable per Section II.B; valves retained their safety function throughout, so no operability-loss consequence node beyond the TS band violation was created. Six previous similar LERs across BFN Units 1, 2, and 3 emitted as stub LER nodes with keys exactly as stated in the narrative (unit-prefixed cycle-year-sequence format), per rule 9."
}